From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 07:56:05 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] net: Rebuild the HTTP cache on http-cache-rules and a
 streaming store

The HTTP cache kept every stored body as a `Vec<u8>` in the net process,
shared with the response handed to the consumer, so net also kept the
decoded body of every response until its fetch ended. Its RFC 9111
rules were hand-written for RFC 7234.

The rules now come from the `http-cache-rules` crate: storability, the
stored and updated header fields, freshness (with `max-age`, `Expires`,
the heuristic, `Age` and `Date`), `Vary`, `stale-while-revalidate`,
`immutable`, the request directives, the validation request and the
merge of a 304. The crate decides and stores nothing; its requirements
are traced to the RFC text and tested, model checked and fuzzed in its
own repository. `http_cache::policy` configures it as a private cache
that stores 206 responses and their bodies encoded.

Servo keeps what belongs to the fetch layer: the cache modes, background
revalidation (only for requests with a client), invalidation after
unsafe methods, ranges, and its own refusals on top of the crate: `Vary:
*`, entries over the store's size limit, 206 without `Content-Range`,
and the storage rule for audio and video of patch 79.

Storage moves behind `CacheStore`, which streams bodies in and out:

  * `TeeBody` copies each frame into the store below the content
    decoder, so the stored body is what came off the wire, and the copy
    is a reference count, not a second allocation. The store's bounded
    queue back-pressures the network.
  * A hit streams the entry through the decoder in chunks.
  * `ResponseBody::Streamed` marks a body delivered to the consumer and
    kept nowhere. Only subresource integrity and preloads still keep the
    whole decoded body (`retains_whole_body`), so net no longer keeps
    media bodies, which patch 79 had to arrange separately.
  * An entry is visible only once its decoded body ended cleanly, so a
    failed, cancelled or truncated body never becomes an entry, which
    patch 80 had to arrange separately. The fetch commits it through a
    `TeeCommit`, not `TeeBody` at the end of the network body: the
    decoder stops reading at the end of a gzip or brotli stream.
  * Every entry carries a random stamp, and the store opens, updates
    and removes an entry only for the stamp it was looked up with, since
    a later entry of the key may reuse its id.
  * `http_cache::inflight` lets one fetch write a key. Once its headers
    show a fresh response, others wait for it and read the committed
    entry, or go to the network if it aborts or, as patch 92 requires,
    while it is suspended. Before that, or if the response needs
    validation, they go to the network as with the old cache, so a slow
    or long-polled response holds up no other fetch. Once the whole body
    has arrived, they wait for any response while the store commits it,
    so a fetch right after another one finds its entry. A fetch does
    not wait for a response that varies on a field in which its own
    request differs, or whose body outgrows what the store accepts. It
    stops waiting when it is cancelled, and goes on from there like any
    cancelled fetch, and it waits 5 s at most.
  * `MemoryStore` is the only store in this patch: byte-bounded, LRU,
    for private browsing, temporary storage, no cache directory, and
    the tests. The SQLite cache goes, and `rusqlite`, `sea-query` and
    `quick_cache` leave `components/net`.

Fixes over the cache of the `http-cache-v2` branch, which this replaces:
a 304 no longer collapses repeated stored fields (a two-line CSP lost a
line) nor freshens on a weak or mismatching strong validator, `Age`
takes `Date` and the request time into account, the RFC 850 and asctime
date formats are understood, and `Vary` compares all field lines. That
branch also never stored a compressed response but stored a gzip body
cut short under clean framing, let a late 304 freshen or remove the
entry that had replaced its own, and made a fetch wait for the whole
body of another fetch's response even when it could not use it, and
wrote the URL's credentials into every entry: the key now leaves them
and the fragment out, and a stored response is served with the
request's URL. An
invalidation through `Location` or `Content-Location` reaches only URIs
of the target's origin (RFC 9111 4.4). Of several matching stored
responses the most recent is used (RFC 9111 4). When a 304 is about
another response than the stored one, the request is made again without
preconditions, and that response is stored.

Prefs: `network_http_cache_size` (an entry count) and
`network_http_disk_cache` (a path) are replaced by
`network_http_memory_cache_size` and `network_http_cache_max_entry_size`,
in bytes; `network_http_disk_cache_size` gains 0 = automatic.

`apply_algorithm_to_response` returned through `unreachable!()` when
the body was not `Done`, which a null body status makes it, and a
streamed body would reach reliably. It now returns a digest that cannot
match, so the fetch fails as `response-null-body.any.js` expects.

`preload/prefetch-document.html`: "different-site document prefetch with
'as=document' should not be consumed" now also fails at times, like its
two siblings. Servo has no HTTP cache partition (#33616), so the fresh
response of a cross-site prefetch answers the later navigation, unless
the navigation starts before the prefetch's response headers arrive and
goes to the network. With `network_http_cache_disabled=true` the subtest
passes and the "same origin ... should be consumed" sibling fails
instead.

Fork note: supersedes patches 79 and 80 (their cache code goes; their
fetch tests stay), and moves the cache side of patch 92 into
`http_cache::inflight`. 79 and 80 stayed on the stack at the 2026-10-22
sync: patch 92 builds on both (it calls 79's `may_store_media_response`
and sets 80's abort flag), so dropping them means reworking 92. Uses
`http-cache-rules` 0.1.0 from crates.io.

Testing: tests/http_cache.rs covers storability, Vary, freshness,
stale-while-revalidate with and without a client, partial entries and
ranges (with the five range tests of the `http-cache-range` branch),
the encoded body, the media storage rule, same-origin invalidation and
the most recent variant. tests/fetch.rs covers a 304 with another strong
validator, a 304 resetting Age, and a 304 with no-store.
tests/http_loader.rs covers a compressed response answered from the
cache, a compressed body cut short, and fetches that do not wait for the
headers of another fetch, a response that needs validation, a variant
of another request or a body too large to store, and that stop waiting
when cancelled or after a while; tests/http_cache.rs also a URL with
credentials and a fragment; tests/cache_store.rs an id reused by another
entry. The fetch tests of
patches 79, 80 and 92 are adapted to entries that appear on commit.

Validation: `./mach test-unit -p servo-net`: 435 tests pass. Negative controls, each run once: without the origin check, the most-recent selection, the 304 exception in `tee` or the suspended-producer wait, `test_invalidation_only_reaches_the_same_origin`, `test_the_most_recent_matching_response_is_used`, `test_a_304_for_another_response_makes_the_request_again_without_preconditions` and `test_fetch_waiting_for_a_suspended_response_uses_the_network` fail. Without the commit on the decoded end, the stamps and the waiting rule, `test_compressed_response_is_answered_from_cache`, `test_compressed_response_cut_short_is_not_stored`, `a_reused_id_is_not_touched_on_behalf_of_its_old_entry` (memory and disk store) and `test_fetch_does_not_wait_for_a_response_that_needs_validation` fail; with waiters waiting from the writer's registration on, `test_fetch_does_not_wait_for_the_headers_of_another_fetch` fails as well, and with waiters waiting for any response the validation one does. With patches 121 to 123: `./mach test-unit --profile checked-release` (1212 tests) and `--doc` pass; `./mach clippy --locked -- -- --deny warnings`, the crown `checked-release` build and the OHOS `checked-release` build with `RUSTFLAGS="-D warnings"` are clean, as is `./mach test-tidy`. WPT, `checked-release`, 8 processes, fetch/http-cache, fetch/range, fetch/stale-while-revalidate, fetch/api, cors, xhr, preload, html/semantics/embedded-content/media-elements and html/semantics/scripting-1, 2140 tests: 2134 as expected, and "Response with Cache-Control: max-age=2592000, public and Pragma: no-cache should not be cached" now passes (expectation removed; the crate's deviation D-2). The other six behave the same alone on ohos-main 091e100215c: src_object_blob.html times out 3 of 8 runs (2 of 8 there), load-removes-queued-error-event.html crashes 5 of 8 (4 of 8 there), media_fragment_seek.html fails and autoplay-default-permissions-policy.https.sub.html passes every time on both, and seek-to-max-value.htm and track-remove-by-setting-innerHTML.html pass 3 of 3 on both. Full WPT, `checked-release`, 12 processes, on the build before the waiting rule was fixed: 58273 of 58348 tests as expected. The other 75 were run again serially, those still unexpected on ohos-main 091e100215c too, and the 12 that differed 3 times alone on both: async_003.htm, async_004.htm and async_005.htm failed every time on the branch only (their script's headers are delayed, and a second fetch of it waited for them), and pass 3 of 3 with the fix; the other 9 behave the same on both. The focused set without scripting-1 on QuickJS (`--js-engine quickjs`), before the review fixes: 1540 of 1545 as expected, three of the same media tests, and two track-element timeouts that pass 3 of 3 alone on both builds. On ohos-main deaca97cabe with `http-cache-rules` 0.1.0 from crates.io (the same files as the path dependency tested above): `./mach test-unit -p servo-net` passes, 435 tests here and 472 with patch 121, and the CI-parity checks above pass again at patch 123, except `indexeddb::engines::sqlite::tests::test_async_operations` of servo-storage, which timed out once and passes 3 of 3 alone. `test_response_cache_status_is_validated` failed 5 of 320 runs, 32 at a time, while the commit of the first response was not waited for, and 0 of 960 after. WPT fetch/http-cache, fetch/range, fetch/stale-while-revalidate, preload and html/semantics/scripting-1, 756 tests: as expected but for execution-timing/077.html, which passes 3 of 3 alone here and fails 1 of 3 alone on ohos-main 091e100215c, and the prefetch-document subtest above, which passed 2 of 3 runs alone; its expectation now allows both. Review fixes (the wait ends on cancellation, does not start for another variant or a body too large to store, and times out; the key leaves out the credentials and fragment of the URL): 440 tests pass here, and 481 with patch 121. Negative controls, each run once: without the cancellation of the wait, `test_a_waiting_fetch_stops_waiting_when_it_is_cancelled` fails; without the `Vary` check, the cap in `TeeBody`, the timeout or the password left out of the key, `test_fetch_does_not_wait_for_a_response_that_varies_from_its_request`, `test_fetch_does_not_wait_for_a_response_too_large_to_store`, `test_a_waiting_fetch_goes_to_the_network_after_a_while` and `test_url_credentials_and_fragment_are_not_part_of_the_key` fail. At patch 123 the CI-parity checks pass again (`test-unit --profile checked-release`: 1221 tests), and WPT fetch/http-cache, fetch/range, fetch/stale-while-revalidate, fetch/api, preload, html/semantics/scripting-1 and html/semantics/embedded-content/media-elements, 1679 tests, run as expected but for four of the media tests above and seek-to-currentTime.html, which passes 3 of 3 alone on both builds. A first version also returned a network error from step 9 of HTTP-network-or-cache fetch for any fetch cancelled before it reached the network. PR CI caught that: requests sent while a document unloads were dropped, and fetch/fetch-later/send-on-discard/send-multiple, both sandbox-top-navigation-child-delivered tests, navigation-timing/nested-unload-timing and navigation-timing/prefetch-transfer-size-executor failed in both attempts of that CI run and in a serial local run. Without it they run as expected in 2 of 2 serial local runs; of the other stable unexpected results of that CI run, window-resize-event.html times out alone on ohos-main 091e100215c too, and test_resource_timing and media-loading-pseudo-classes-in-has.sub.html behave the same alone on both builds and are flaky upstream.
